1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,500
Compressed gas cylinders, proper handling and storage.

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:18,290
Before storing, handling or using compressed gas cylinders, complete Laboratory Safety Compressed Gas Cylinder training through Workday Learning.

3
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:23,290
A compressed gas cylinder is a gas or mixture of gases contained under high pressure.

4
00:00:23,290 --> 00:00:28,290
It is important to be aware of the hazards regarding compressed gas cylinders.

5
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:34,290
Cylinders can come in different shapes and sizes with removable or built-in caps.

6
00:00:34,290 --> 00:00:47,220
Familiarize yourself with the safety data sheets for the gas prior to storing and handling.

7
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:52,220
Make sure you understand the hazards as well as the chemical and physical properties of the material.

8
00:00:52,220 --> 00:01:09,840
Accidents can occur due to improper storage, unexpected release or leaks, incorrect installation, damage to the cylinder and improper handling.

9
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,840
Follow these compressed gas cylinder storage rules.

10
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:17,840
Store in a cool, dry and well-ventilated space.

11
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:24,840
Store flammable and oxidizing gases at least 20 feet apart unless an approved partition has been installed.

12
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:31,840
Store in an upright position, secured above the midpoint but below the shoulder with the safety cap secured.

13
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:38,250
Never store gas cylinders in exits or egress routes.

14
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:55,840
In damp areas, exposed to direct sunlight or heat, near salts or corrosives, next to incompatible materials for longer than one year without use.

15
00:01:55,840 --> 00:02:02,840
When working in a lab, wear the minimum required PPE, eye protection, lab coat, gloves and fully enclosed shoes.

16
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:10,500
Before transporting the cylinder, confirm the cap is on and tightened.

17
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:14,500
This cap protects the cylinder's valve in the event of a bump or fall.

18
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:18,500
Never lift a cylinder from the cap or drag a cylinder across the floor.

19
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:28,250
Do not hand roll cylinders for more than a few feet. They are hard to control and dropping the cylinder is very dangerous.

20
00:02:28,250 --> 00:02:35,250
Do not try to catch a cylinder if it is falling. Many injuries are caused by trying to catch heavy objects.

21
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:39,250
Transport the cylinder by securing it to an appropriate cart.

22
00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:44,250
Only transport cylinders secured in an upright position and in university vehicles.

23
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:48,250
Do not transport cylinders in passenger compartments.

24
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:52,250
Use an elevator or a dumbwaiter to move cylinders between floors.

25
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:59,120
But do not ride with it.

26
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:12,920
Attach a sign to the cylinder, telling others not to use the elevator during the cylinder's trip.

27
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:17,920
For more information about gas cylinders, check out the Gas Cylinder Safety Guidelines Manual linked below.

28
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,920
If you have questions, please contact ISUEHNS.
